{"p":"/animation/events","t":"Animation events","d":"Animation Events are markers across the timeline span in the Animation Editor.","h":"<p>You can define animation <strong>event markers</strong> across the timeline span and use\n<a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AnimationTrack/AnimationTrack:GetMarkerReachedSignal\"><code>GetMarkerReachedSignal()</code></a>\nto detect those markers as the animation runs.</p>\n<p>By default, the event track isn't visible. To show the event track:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Navigate to the right of the timeline and click the settings icon. A pop-up menu displays.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" /></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Select <strong>Show Animation Events</strong>. This opens the <strong>Animation Events</strong> bar directly below the media and\n   playback controls.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" /></p>\n<h2>Create events</h2>\n<p>Event markers are visual indicators of where an animation event begins. After you create an event\nmarker, you can move it to any frame position on the timeline.</p>\n<p>To create a new event marker:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Navigate to the timeline, then click-and-drag the scrubber to the frame position where the event should occur.</li>\n<li>Navigate to the <strong>event track</strong>, then click the <strong>Edit Animation Events</strong> button.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" /></p>\n<ol>\n<li>In the dialog, click <strong>+ Add Event</strong>, then enter an event name.</li>\n<li><span class=\"chip\">OPTIONAL</span> In the <strong>Parameter</strong> field, enter a parameter string for the event.</li>\n<li>Click the <strong>Save</strong> button. In the events bar within the timeline, a new marker symbol displays at the frame position.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" /></p>\n<h2>Detect events</h2>\n<p>To detect animation events in a <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/LocalScript\"><code>LocalScript</code></a>,\nconnect a function to the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AnimationTrack/AnimationTrack:GetMarkerReachedSignal\"><code>GetMarkerReachedSignal()</code></a> function of <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AnimationTrack\"><code>AnimationTrack</code></a>. For example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-lua\">local Players = game:GetService(&quot;Players&quot;)\n\nlocal player = Players.LocalPlayer\nlocal character = player.Character or player.CharacterAdded:Wait()\nlocal humanoid = character:WaitForChild(&quot;Humanoid&quot;)\nlocal animator = humanoid:WaitForChild(&quot;Animator&quot;)\n\n-- Create new &quot;Animation&quot; instance\nlocal walkAnim = Instance.new(&quot;Animation&quot;)\n-- Set its &quot;AnimationId&quot; to the corresponding animation asset ID\nwalkAnim.AnimationId = &quot;rbxassetid://5432167890&quot;\n\n-- Load animation onto the animator\nlocal walkAnimTrack = animator:LoadAnimation(walkAnim)\n\n-- Connect &quot;GetMarkerReachedSignal&quot; event to a specific named keyframe\nwalkAnimTrack:GetMarkerReachedSignal(&quot;FootStep&quot;):Connect(function(paramString)\n    print(paramString)\nend)\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"callout c-info\">\n<p>You can specify a <strong>Parameter</strong> value for any event marker within the animation editor. This lets you pass a custom <strong>string</strong> (single value, comma-separated string, etc.) to the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/AnimationTrack/AnimationTrack:GetMarkerReachedSignal\"><code>GetMarkerReachedSignal()</code></a> method, as illustrated by the <code>paramString</code> argument in the code example above. This string can then be parsed or converted, if necessary, and used for whatever action you wish to perform in the event.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Duplicate events</h2>\n<p>As you create events, they become available for usage throughout the whole\nanimation, not only at the frame position where you originally created them. For\ninstance, you can create a <code>FootStep</code> event marker at the point where a\ncharacter's <strong>left</strong> foot touches down, then use the same event when the\ncharacter's <strong>right</strong> foot touches down.</p>\n<p>To duplicate an event:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Right-click an <strong>event marker</strong> in the event bar, then select <strong>Copy Selected</strong> from the contextual menu.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" /></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Right-click at another time along the timeline and select <strong>Paste Events</strong>. The copied event inserts at the time position closest to where you click.</li>\n<li>\n<p><span class=\"chip\">OPTIONAL</span> If the original event uses a <strong>parameter</strong> but the duplicated event should use a modified parameter, perform the following steps:</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Right-click the duplicated event marker and select <strong>Edit Animation Event</strong> from the contextual menu. The <strong>Edit Animation Events</strong> dialog\n   displays.</p>\n</li>\n<li>Make your changes and click the <strong>Save</strong> button.</li>\n</ol>","kid":{"simple":"Animation events are named markers you place at specific moments in an animation, like the exact frame a foot hits the ground. While the animation plays, a script can listen for that marker and do something right then, like play a footstep sound or spawn a dust puff. This keeps sounds and effects perfectly in sync with movement. You can also attach a short text value to each marker to give your script extra info.","analogy":"It is like cue marks in a school play script: when the actor reaches the line marked \"thunder\", the sound crew plays the thunder sound.","keyPoints":["The event track is hidden at first. Click the settings icon to the right of the timeline and choose Show Animation Events.","To add one: move the scrubber to the frame, click Edit Animation Events, click + Add Event, type a name, optionally add a Parameter, and click Save.","Once made, an event name can be reused anywhere in the animation, like a `FootStep` marker for both the left and right foot.","Copy a marker with right-click, Copy Selected, then right-click elsewhere and choose Paste Events.","In a LocalScript, use `AnimationTrack:GetMarkerReachedSignal(\"Name\")` and `:Connect` a function to run code when that marker is reached.","The marker's Parameter text is passed to your function, so you can send extra info like `\"left\"` or `\"right\"`."],"terms":{"event marker":"A small symbol on the timeline that marks when an animation event happens.","parameter":"An optional text value saved with the marker, like `\"left\"`, that your script receives when the marker is hit.","AnimationTrack":"A playable copy of an animation loaded onto a character. You call `:Play()` on it and listen to its markers.","Animator":"The object inside a `Humanoid` that loads and plays animations.","signal":"Something that fires when an event happens. You `:Connect` a function to it to react.","asset ID":"The number Roblox gives your published animation, used as `rbxassetid://` plus the number."},"example":{"title":"Print which foot stepped","place":"LocalScript in StarterCharacterScripts","code":"local Players = game:GetService(\"Players\")\n\nlocal player = Players.LocalPlayer\nlocal character = player.Character or player.CharacterAdded:Wait()\nlocal humanoid = character:WaitForChild(\"Humanoid\")\nlocal animator = humanoid:WaitForChild(\"Animator\")\n\n-- Point to your published walk animation (replace the ID)\nlocal walkAnim = Instance.new(\"Animation\")\nwalkAnim.AnimationId = \"rbxassetid://5432167890\"\n\n-- Load it so we get an AnimationTrack\nlocal walkTrack = animator:LoadAnimation(walkAnim)\n\n-- Run this every time the \"FootStep\" marker is reached\nwalkTrack:GetMarkerReachedSignal(\"FootStep\"):Connect(function(paramString)\n\tprint(\"Step with:\", paramString) -- e.g. \"left\" or \"right\"\nend)\n\nwalkTrack:Play()","explain":["We find the player's character, its `Humanoid`, and the `Animator` inside it.","We make an `Animation` object and set its `AnimationId` to the published animation's ID.","`LoadAnimation` turns it into an `AnimationTrack` we can play.","`GetMarkerReachedSignal(\"FootStep\")` gives a signal for that marker name; our function runs each time it is reached.","`paramString` is the Parameter text you typed for that marker in the editor.","`walkTrack:Play()` starts the animation so the markers can fire."]},"watchOut":["The marker name in the script must match the editor exactly, including capitals. `\"Footstep\"` will not catch `FootStep`.","Forgetting to republish the animation after adding events. The game uses the published version.","Not seeing the event track. Turn on Show Animation Events from the timeline settings.","Pasted events land where you right-click, not where the scrubber is."]}}